{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JSFhh6MpXu5U4F56caDZRD","type":"tool_result","content":"## 5. Input\n\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jum\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come \n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\n- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else nu\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.cl\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a fi\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. fa\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'res\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, r\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talk\n- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) \n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\n- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\n- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\n- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers\n\n## Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js)\n\n- `new E.Mode7(o = {}) → mode7`: new E.Mode7({ floor, size, outside, wrap, sky, fog, far }): a Mode 7 view (the manual above)\n- `mode7.add(x, y, z, fn)`: gather a billboard: fn(g, sx, sy, k) draws it with its foot at (sx, sy), k screen pixels per unit\n- `mode7.begin(rect = {}, cam = this.cam)`: set up the projection for a camera (default this.cam) and a rectangle of the screen ({ W, H, x, y, cx, cy }: its size, its top left and its centre; focal: screen pixels per unit at a distance of one, instead of the fov)\n- `mode7.chase(b, o = {})`: the camera behind a body each step: back units behind it, h high, looking along its angle (o.angle to set one)\n- `mode7.draw(r, cam = this.cam)`: draw the view as an overlay over r's frame (in a split screen, r.viewport's part): sky, floor, fog, then the billboards far to near (gathered with add, rig, sprite; they are used up)\n- `mode7.drawFloor(g, vp)`: draw the floor below the horizon in the rectangle ({ x, y, w, h }), a row at a time, and the fog over it\n- `mode7.drawSky(g, r, vp)`: the sky above the horizon: a Backdrop turned with the heading, or a gradient of two colors\n- `mode7.fullRes(game, on)`: draw at the screen's own pixels (on, called each step: sharper, slower) or the game's again (off); returns the pixel size the game had (1 when it had no other)\n- `mode7.project(x, y, z = 0)`: a world point -> [screen x, screen y, screen pixels per unit there], or null at or behind the camera\n- `mode7.refresh()`: forget the floor's baked texture (after changing the map or the function: it bakes again)\n- `mode7.rig(rig, x, y, z = 0)`: gather a rig, drawn by the engine at the size its distance gives, from the camera's side (it faces rig.facing)\n- `mode7.rowDepth(sy)`: a screen row's floor distance ahead of the camera, or 0 at or above the horizon\n- `mode7.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather one of the engine's shapes (r.shape's: 'barrel', 'tree', ...; the full engine), seen from the camera's side at its distance's size: o.facing (radians), o.size (1), o.params and o.mats (as r.shape's: a door's angle, a crate's wood)\n- `mode7.sprite(spr, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather a sprite (E.sprite, or E.anim's frames at o.t, the game's time by default), its foot at the point: o.scale (world units a sprite pixel, 1), o.flip\n- `mode7.toFloor(sx, sy)`: a screen point -> the floor point under it [x, y], or null above the horizon\n- `mode7.view(k)`: the engine view a rig is drawn with at k pixels per unit, seen from the camera's heading (sixteenths, reused) and pitch (whole degrees: a bobbing camera makes no new view each frame)\n- `mode7.cam`: the camera ({ x, y, h, angle, pitch, fov })\n- `mode7.horizon = 0`: the screen row of the horizon in the last drawing\n- `mode7.items = []`: the billboards gathered for the next draw\n- `mode7.o`: its options\n\n## Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js)\n\n- `new E.Physics(o = {}) → physics`: new E.Physics({ gravity, substeps, iterations, sleep, on, cell }): a world of bodies (see the manual)\n- `physics.add(d)`: a body from its definition (see the manual)\n- `physics.arc(x, y, r, a0, a1, o = {})`: a curved rail: a chain round (x, y) at radius r from angle a0 to a1 (radians; o.n segments)\n- `physics.box(x, y, w, h, o)`: a box w x h about its middle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.bumper(x, y, r, o = {})`: a bumper: a static round post that kicks a ball away (kick: pixels a second)\n- `physics.chain(points, o = {})`: static segments through the points (o.closed: back to the first); returns them\n- `physics.circle(x, y, r, o)`: a circle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.draw(g, to = (x, y) => [x, y], color = '#7cf2a0')`: outline every body into g (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: the plane on the screen; its identity by default)\n- `physics.flipper(x, y, o = {})`: a flipper: a kinematic tapered bar from its pivot (x, y); flip(on) swings it up or back (see the manual)\n- `physics.hash()`: a number for the world now: every body's place, angle and speed (a proof's hash)\n- `physics.kicker(x, y, o = {})`: a kicker: a sensor that catches a ball, holds it, then fires it (a saucer, a kickback); k.catch.armed\n- `physics.poly(x, y, points, o)`: a convex polygon, its points round x, y (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.query(x, y)`: the bodies over a point\n- `physics.ray(a, b, mask = 0xffff)`: the nearest body along the line from a to b (sensors aside; mask: the layers it sees): { body, x, y, t } or null\n- `physics.remove(b)`: take a body out\n- `physics.segment(a, b, o)`: a static segment from a to b (o.r: its thickness's half)\n- `physics.slingshot(a, b, o = {})`: a slingshot: a static segment that kicks a ball off it\n- `physics.step(dt)`: advance by dt seconds (from the game's fixed step), in substeps\n- `physics.wake()`: wake every body (after a change to gravity, say)\n- `physics.bodies = []`: every body in the order added\n- `physics.contacts = []`: the last substep's ({ a, b, nx, ny, pts, fresh })\n- `physics.gravity`: [gx, gy], pixels a second a second\n- `physics.iterations`: the solver's passes\n- `physics.on`: its events\n- `physics.sleep`: bodies at rest sleep\n- `physics.substeps`: steps per step(dt)\n\n## Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js)\n\n- `new E.Rhythm(game, o = {}) → rhythm` †: new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual\n- `rhythm.accuracy` †: how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4\n- `rhythm.ahead(call = false)` †: the notes to show now: [{ note, dt, dtEnd }] (seconds to the note's time and to its end; a little past the line for the missed, the held while held); call: the calls instead\n- `rhythm.draw(r, o = {})` †: draw the highway on the overlay (see the manual)\n- `rhythm.grade` †: the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D'\n- `rhythm.hit(lane)` †: a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held)\n- `rhythm.release(lane)` †: a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops\n- `rhythm.reset()` †: back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given\n- `rhythm.start()` †: play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here\n- `rhythm.total` †: the notes to play (not the calls)\n- `rhythm.update()` †: each step, after the input: the lanes' actions pressed and let go, calls sounded, notes missed, holds kept, the end\n- `rhythm.combo = 0` †: the notes hit in a row\n- `rhythm.counts` †: { perfect, great, good, miss }\n- `rhythm.done = false` †: the song is over, or failed\n- `rhythm.down` †: each lane held now\n- `rhythm.failed = false` †: the life ran out\n- `rhythm.game` †: its game\n- `rhythm.lanes` †: the actions, one a lane\n- `rhythm.last = null` †: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }\n- `rhythm.lead` †: seconds of notes shown ahead\n- `rhythm.life` †: the gauge 0..1\n- `rhythm.maxCombo = 0` †: the longest combo\n- `rhythm.notes` †: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms })\n- `rhythm.on` †: { judge, fail, done }\n- `rhythm.score = 0` †: the points\n- `rhythm.song` †: the song charted\n- `rhythm.started = false` †: start() was called\n- `rhythm.voice` †: the calls' sound\n- `rhythm.windows` †: { perfect, great, good } in ms either side of a note\n\n## Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js)\n\n- `new E.Road(o = {}) → road`: new E.Road({ segment, width, lanes, rumble, draw, fov, height, colors, fog, sky }): a road (the manual above)\n- `road.add(n, o = {})`: n segments more, bending (curve) and climbing (hill: units up, - down), eased in and out; o.tunnel, o.colors\n- `road.ahead(h = this.o.height, r)`: how far past the camera the player's car stands when drawn at the screen's bottom, for a camera h over the road; square, that depends on the screen's shape: give r (draw's), and draw the camera that far behind the car (keep the car's own place along the road as it was: the game plays the same on any s\n- `road.curveAt(z)`: how hard the road bends at a distance\n- `road.draw(r, v = {})`: draw the road over r's frame (an overlay; r.viewport's part in a split screen): { z, x, h, turn, cars }\n- `road.hits(z, x, w)`: the scenery a body w half roads wide at (z, x) touches\n- `road.place(z, offset, what, scale = 8)`: scenery at distance z, offset across: what = an E.sprite or E.anim (scale: world units a pixel, 8) or { draw(g, sx, sy, scale), w }\n- `road.project(z, x, y = 0)`: a point (distance z, across x in half roads, height y over the road) -> [sx, sy, scale] for the camera last drawn, or null behind it\n- `road.seg(z)`: the segment at a distance (it wraps)\n- `road.yAt(z)`: the road's height at a distance\n- `road.depth`: the camera's depth (1 / tan(fov / 2): a unit that far away fills half the screen's width)\n- `road.length = 0`: the lap in world units\n- `road.o`: its options (colors over the defaults)\n- `road.segs = []`: its segments ({ i, z, y0, y1, curve, tunnel, colors, things })","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a88ba50-da75-48c3-bde0-396ebfab72e9","uuid":"4c3a186d-b67d-4c67-8d9b-31d9f07b66dd","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:42.632Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## 5. Input\n\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jum\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come \n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\n- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else nu\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.cl\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a fi\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. fa\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'res\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, r\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talk\n- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) \n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\n- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\n- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\n- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers\n\n## Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js)\n\n- `new E.Mode7(o = {}) → mode7`: new E.Mode7({ floor, size, outside, wrap, sky, fog, far }): a Mode 7 view (the manual above)\n- `mode7.add(x, y, z, fn)`: gather a billboard: fn(g, sx, sy, k) draws it with its foot at (sx, sy), k screen pixels per unit\n- `mode7.begin(rect = {}, cam = this.cam)`: set up the projection for a camera (default this.cam) and a rectangle of the screen ({ W, H, x, y, cx, cy }: its size, its top left and its centre; focal: screen pixels per unit at a distance of one, instead of the fov)\n- `mode7.chase(b, o = {})`: the camera behind a body each step: back units behind it, h high, looking along its angle (o.angle to set one)\n- `mode7.draw(r, cam = this.cam)`: draw the view as an overlay over r's frame (in a split screen, r.viewport's part): sky, floor, fog, then the billboards far to near (gathered with add, rig, sprite; they are used up)\n- `mode7.drawFloor(g, vp)`: draw the floor below the horizon in the rectangle ({ x, y, w, h }), a row at a time, and the fog over it\n- `mode7.drawSky(g, r, vp)`: the sky above the horizon: a Backdrop turned with the heading, or a gradient of two colors\n- `mode7.fullRes(game, on)`: draw at the screen's own pixels (on, called each step: sharper, slower) or the game's again (off); returns the pixel size the game had (1 when it had no other)\n- `mode7.project(x, y, z = 0)`: a world point -> [screen x, screen y, screen pixels per unit there], or null at or behind the camera\n- `mode7.refresh()`: forget the floor's baked texture (after changing the map or the function: it bakes again)\n- `mode7.rig(rig, x, y, z = 0)`: gather a rig, drawn by the engine at the size its distance gives, from the camera's side (it faces rig.facing)\n- `mode7.rowDepth(sy)`: a screen row's floor distance ahead of the camera, or 0 at or above the horizon\n- `mode7.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather one of the engine's shapes (r.shape's: 'barrel', 'tree', ...; the full engine), seen from the camera's side at its distance's size: o.facing (radians), o.size (1), o.params and o.mats (as r.shape's: a door's angle, a crate's wood)\n- `mode7.sprite(spr, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather a sprite (E.sprite, or E.anim's frames at o.t, the game's time by default), its foot at the point: o.scale (world units a sprite pixel, 1), o.flip\n- `mode7.toFloor(sx, sy)`: a screen point -> the floor point under it [x, y], or null above the horizon\n- `mode7.view(k)`: the engine view a rig is drawn with at k pixels per unit, seen from the camera's heading (sixteenths, reused) and pitch (whole degrees: a bobbing camera makes no new view each frame)\n- `mode7.cam`: the camera ({ x, y, h, angle, pitch, fov })\n- `mode7.horizon = 0`: the screen row of the horizon in the last drawing\n- `mode7.items = []`: the billboards gathered for the next draw\n- `mode7.o`: its options\n\n## Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js)\n\n- `new E.Physics(o = {}) → physics`: new E.Physics({ gravity, substeps, iterations, sleep, on, cell }): a world of bodies (see the manual)\n- `physics.add(d)`: a body from its definition (see the manual)\n- `physics.arc(x, y, r, a0, a1, o = {})`: a curved rail: a chain round (x, y) at radius r from angle a0 to a1 (radians; o.n segments)\n- `physics.box(x, y, w, h, o)`: a box w x h about its middle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.bumper(x, y, r, o = {})`: a bumper: a static round post that kicks a ball away (kick: pixels a second)\n- `physics.chain(points, o = {})`: static segments through the points (o.closed: back to the first); returns them\n- `physics.circle(x, y, r, o)`: a circle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.draw(g, to = (x, y) => [x, y], color = '#7cf2a0')`: outline every body into g (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: the plane on the screen; its identity by default)\n- `physics.flipper(x, y, o = {})`: a flipper: a kinematic tapered bar from its pivot (x, y); flip(on) swings it up or back (see the manual)\n- `physics.hash()`: a number for the world now: every body's place, angle and speed (a proof's hash)\n- `physics.kicker(x, y, o = {})`: a kicker: a sensor that catches a ball, holds it, then fires it (a saucer, a kickback); k.catch.armed\n- `physics.poly(x, y, points, o)`: a convex polygon, its points round x, y (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.query(x, y)`: the bodies over a point\n- `physics.ray(a, b, mask = 0xffff)`: the nearest body along the line from a to b (sensors aside; mask: the layers it sees): { body, x, y, t } or null\n- `physics.remove(b)`: take a body out\n- `physics.segment(a, b, o)`: a static segment from a to b (o.r: its thickness's half)\n- `physics.slingshot(a, b, o = {})`: a slingshot: a static segment that kicks a ball off it\n- `physics.step(dt)`: advance by dt seconds (from the game's fixed step), in substeps\n- `physics.wake()`: wake every body (after a change to gravity, say)\n- `physics.bodies = []`: every body in the order added\n- `physics.contacts = []`: the last substep's ({ a, b, nx, ny, pts, fresh })\n- `physics.gravity`: [gx, gy], pixels a second a second\n- `physics.iterations`: the solver's passes\n- `physics.on`: its events\n- `physics.sleep`: bodies at rest sleep\n- `physics.substeps`: steps per step(dt)\n\n## Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js)\n\n- `new E.Rhythm(game, o = {}) → rhythm` †: new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual\n- `rhythm.accuracy` †: how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4\n- `rhythm.ahead(call = false)` †: the notes to show now: [{ note, dt, dtEnd }] (seconds to the note's time and to its end; a little past the line for the missed, the held while held); call: the calls instead\n- `rhythm.draw(r, o = {})` †: draw the highway on the overlay (see the manual)\n- `rhythm.grade` †: the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D'\n- `rhythm.hit(lane)` †: a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held)\n- `rhythm.release(lane)` †: a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops\n- `rhythm.reset()` †: back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given\n- `rhythm.start()` †: play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here\n- `rhythm.total` †: the notes to play (not the calls)\n- `rhythm.update()` †: each step, after the input: the lanes' actions pressed and let go, calls sounded, notes missed, holds kept, the end\n- `rhythm.combo = 0` †: the notes hit in a row\n- `rhythm.counts` †: { perfect, great, good, miss }\n- `rhythm.done = false` †: the song is over, or failed\n- `rhythm.down` †: each lane held now\n- `rhythm.failed = false` †: the life ran out\n- `rhythm.game` †: its game\n- `rhythm.lanes` †: the actions, one a lane\n- `rhythm.last = null` †: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }\n- `rhythm.lead` †: seconds of notes shown ahead\n- `rhythm.life` †: the gauge 0..1\n- `rhythm.maxCombo = 0` †: the longest combo\n- `rhythm.notes` †: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms })\n- `rhythm.on` †: { judge, fail, done }\n- `rhythm.score = 0` †: the points\n- `rhythm.song` †: the song charted\n- `rhythm.started = false` †: start() was called\n- `rhythm.voice` †: the calls' sound\n- `rhythm.windows` †: { perfect, great, good } in ms either side of a note\n\n## Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js)\n\n- `new E.Road(o = {}) → road`: new E.Road({ segment, width, lanes, rumble, draw, fov, height, colors, fog, sky }): a road (the manual above)\n- `road.add(n, o = {})`: n segments more, bending (curve) and climbing (hill: units up, - down), eased in and out; o.tunnel, o.colors\n- `road.ahead(h = this.o.height, r)`: how far past the camera the player's car stands when drawn at the screen's bottom, for a camera h over the road; square, that depends on the screen's shape: give r (draw's), and draw the camera that far behind the car (keep the car's own place along the road as it was: the game plays the same on any s\n- `road.curveAt(z)`: how hard the road bends at a distance\n- `road.draw(r, v = {})`: draw the road over r's frame (an overlay; r.viewport's part in a split screen): { z, x, h, turn, cars }\n- `road.hits(z, x, w)`: the scenery a body w half roads wide at (z, x) touches\n- `road.place(z, offset, what, scale = 8)`: scenery at distance z, offset across: what = an E.sprite or E.anim (scale: world units a pixel, 8) or { draw(g, sx, sy, scale), w }\n- `road.project(z, x, y = 0)`: a point (distance z, across x in half roads, height y over the road) -> [sx, sy, scale] for the camera last drawn, or null behind it\n- `road.seg(z)`: the segment at a distance (it wraps)\n- `road.yAt(z)`: the road's height at a distance\n- `road.depth`: the camera's depth (1 / tan(fov / 2): a unit that far away fills half the screen's width)\n- `road.length = 0`: the lap in world units\n- `road.o`: its options (colors over the defaults)\n- `road.segs = []`: its segments ({ i, z, y0, y1, curve, tunnel, colors, things })","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JSFhh6MpXu5U4F56caDZRD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjDGNFFNNCwAhD2YT1z8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017mKiE98DAigX3ahe81iuiy","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 457,590p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 625,647p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 1064,1097p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-260","description":"Read the game, saves and audio index sections"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4229,"cache_read_input_tokens":111961,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4229},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a88ba50-da75-48c3-bde0-396ebfab72e9","uuid":"3ee3d8ad-a6ff-432f-8b70-3517b86d3b2d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:43.715Z","request_id":"req_011CgjDGMxtGZeoLGArx4jha","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_017mKiE98DAigX3ahe81iuiy":{"command":"sed -n 457,590p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 625,647p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 1064,1097p API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-260","description":"Read the game, saves and audio index sections"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017mKiE98DAigX3ahe81iuiy","type":"tool_result","content":"## 10. Game\n\n- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, live: false, id, pace, camera })\n- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n- `new E.Game({ draw })`: true (headless): draw runs each advance on a stand-in renderer, its errors in game.errors\n- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n- `new E.Game({ live })`: true: a world that never stops for one player (online, a shared screen): a pause opens the menus over it, the scene's update goes on (game.paused says a menu is up) and the menu holds that player's input back\n- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n- `game.H`: the screen's height in game pixels (W is its width)\n- `game.W`: internal buffer size in pixels (HUD layout)\n- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (\n- `game.after(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true } (a site's clock, a watcher of the whole game: it outlives scenes, as a kept tween does). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are Strin\n- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); els\n- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the\n- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n- `game.every(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true }. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power '\n- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', '\n- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, \n- `game.hook(kind, fn)` †: a function of the game's own on the engine's clock: kind 'input' fn(dt) every step, paused or not, after the input is read (a host's guard, an idle timer); 'step' fn(dt) every update step in play, before the scene\n- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings')\n- `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse's point on the ground: [x, y] in world units (an array, not { x, y }), or null with no mouse over the canvas\n- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game \n- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n- `game.players` †: the players: [game.input (player 1), ...the ones who joined (game.allowJoin, game.addPlayer: section 10j)]; each an input with player (1..4), name ('P1'), color (E.PLAYER_COLORS) and device. A game of one: [game.input]\n- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits\n- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles\n- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n- `game.shaking` †: how hard the screen shakes now (0 still, up to 10): shake() adds to it and it dies away. A camera of the game's own (a 3D world's) shakes by it\n- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [t\n- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pa\n- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they\n- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides \n- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(ta\n- `game.upright`: true while the game's screen (r.W x r.H) is upright, taller than 1.3 times its width: a phone held upright, with expand: true (a fixed res keeps its own shape on any phone, so it is never upright there). A scene's camera.t\n- `game.viewports` †: a split screen's viewports ({ i, x, y, w, h, cam, focus(x, y, z), follow(target, { z }): game.splitScreen), or [] on one screen\n- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.\n- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n- `game.alpha = .5`: the fraction of a fixed step (0..1) of real time not yet run: interpolate drawing with it if motion must be smooth at any refresh rate. The accumulator starts half a step in, so frame times that wobble keep a steady ste\n- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n- `game.baseView` †: the chosen view without zoom and turn\n- `game.bootError = null`: the error a boot step failed with (the game did not start), or null\n- `game.booted`: a promise of the game once its boot (start's boot option, section 10c) has run and it has started: await it in a test whose boot steps return promises\n- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits\n- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n- `game.disposed = true`: true after dispose()\n- `game.drawEvery = 1`: 1, or 2 while a fast display (a 120 Hz phone) refreshes quicker than the game can draw (a frame's cost, or refreshes missed: its GPU behind): every other refresh is drawn, evenly, instead of random ones missed (the u\n- `game.errors = []`: the errors caught in update and draw, each { where, scene, message, stack }, once per kind\n- `game.fadeTime = .22`: seconds for the dithered fade between scenes (0 = instant cuts)\n- `game.fps = 60`: frames drawn per second over the last half second of real time\n- `game.fullscreen`: full screen: game.fullscreen.toggle(), .on, .supported\n- `game.gpu = null` †: the WebGPU lighting after enableGPU(), or null\n- `game.headless`: true for a game with no display (o.headless, or no DOM: Node)\n- `game.hitstop = 0`: seconds of hit-stop left (freeze: a scene's entry ends it)\n- `game.hz = 60`: the display's refresh rate, measured\n- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n- `game.live` †: true (o.live): a pause opens the menus over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player): paused is true and the scene's update goes on, the menu holding the player's own input back\n- `game.o`: the options the game was made with, the defaults filled in (view, res, expand, minW, minH, maxW, maxH, bg, step, input, loop, headless)\n- `game.particles`: dust, sparks, debris and text in the world\n- `game.pauseOverlay = true`: false hides the engine's PAUSED overlay (a game drawing its own pause screen)\n- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n- `game.r`: the renderer handed to draw(r) (null when headless)\n- `game.real = 0`: seconds the game has stepped through, pauses and hit-stop included (blinking HUD, notes)\n- `game.reduceMotion`: the player asked for less motion (prefers-reduced-motion): shake and flashes are softened\n- `game.scene = null`: the scene running\n- `game.sceneName = null`: its name ('main' for a game started with one scene)\n- `game.scenes = null`: the scenes given to start(), by name\n- `game.screen`: the low-resolution screen (its W, H, scale and buffer)\n- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n- `game.shell = null` †: the E.Shell over this game (an HTML interface: screens and HUD), or null\n- `game.stats`: per-frame timings (ms) and counts, for profiling overlays\n- `game.stats.updateMs`, `game.stats.renderMs`: milliseconds the last frame spent updating and drawing\n- `game.stats.frameMs`: the real time since the last frame\n- `game.stats.steps`: the updates it ran\n- `game.stats.items`: what it queued for drawing\n- `game.stats.actors`: actors drawn\n- `game.stats.culled`: actors skipped off screen\n- `game.time = 0`: game seconds since start (it stops during pause and hit-stop, and slows with timeScale)\n- `game.timeScale = 1`: game speed, 1 normal, .25 slow motion, 0 stopped (it outlives a scene change: a game's speed setting; restore a slow motion with a kept timer)\n- `game.view`: the view the game draws in now (the chosen one, with zoom and turn: read it, change it with setView)\n- `game.views`: views this game is designed for (view cycling and the checker use it). null = every view\n- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n- `E.PLAYER_COLORS: #4a8aff, #ff4a4a, #4ad860, #ffd040` †: the players' colors: player 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow (game.players)\n- `E.showError(title, e)`: show an error in the red box at the top of the page (the engine does it for errors in update and draw); a click hides it. Before play has started it shows on the boot screen instead, as a report a player can scree\n\n## 10b. Standard features\n\n- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a vali\n- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)\n- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n- `fullscreen.enter(quiet)`: go full screen. Browsers allow it only from a tap, click or key press: call it from one (a button, the menu, a key's update step). Returns a promise of whether it worked (a refusal is kept in failed and noted on\n- `fullscreen.exit()`: leave full screen (Escape and the phone's back gesture do it too)\n- `fullscreen.on`: true while the page is full screen\n- `fullscreen.standalone`: true when the page was opened from a home screen as an app (no browser bars: nothing to go full screen from)\n- `fullscreen.supported`: true where this page can go full screen (not an iPhone, which has Add to Home Screen instead; not a frame without permission; not headless)\n- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itself only while they haven't turned it off\n- `fullscreen.failed = null`: game: the game it serves; failed: why the last request was refused (a message), or null\n\n## 10c. Boot\n\n## 10k. Save slots\n\n- `new E.Saves(game, o) → saves` †: game.saves (made when first read): o is menu.saves ({ slots, version, migrate, write, read })\n- `saves.any` †: is there any save to load (a title's Continue)?\n- `saves.autosave(o)` †: save to the autosave (what write() gives)\n- `saves.copy(from, to)` †: copy a slot's save onto another (over what is there); false when the first is empty\n- `saves.erase(slot)` †: erase a slot\n- `saves.get(slot)` †: a slot's save as it is stored, checked: null when empty; { slot, damaged: true } when it can't be read; { newer: true } from a newer version of the game; else { slot, data, place, level, time, at (the date saved), ver\n- `saves.latest()` †: the most recent save that loads, or null (a title's Continue loads it)\n- `saves.list()` †: every slot, the autosave first: [{ slot, empty, place, level, time, at, damaged, newer }] (a menu's rows)\n- `saves.load(slot)` †: load a slot: its data brought up to this version (migrate), played set from it, read(save) called. Returns the save ({ data, place, level, time, slot }), or null when the slot is empty, damaged or from a newer versio\n- `saves.save(slot, o)` †: save to a slot (0.., or 'auto'): o ({ data, place, level }) or what write() gives. Returns true when it is kept (in storage, or for the visit when storage is blocked: blocked tells), false when there was nothing t\n- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game, menu: false)\n- `saves.blocked = false` †: storage refused a save (they last for the visit)\n- `saves.canSave = true` †: the pause menu offers Save (a JRPG turns it off away from save points)\n- `saves.game` †: its game\n- `saves.migrate` †: an older version's data brought up to this one (or null: kept as it is)\n- `saves.played = 0` †: seconds played in the game's scenes (paused time and titles left out), saved with each save and set by a load\n- `saves.read` †: what a load does with { data, place, level, time, slot }\n- `saves.slots` †: how many slots besides the autosave (3)\n- `saves.version` †: the game's version of its data (1), saved with each save\n- `saves.write` †: what a save holds ({ data, place, level })\n\n## 20. Audio\n\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (m\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, {\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playi\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a88ba50-da75-48c3-bde0-396ebfab72e9","uuid":"1edf74c2-af7e-4c68-8902-26b2acd5bd75","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:43.741Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## 10. Game\n\n- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, live: false, id, pace, camera })\n- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n- `new E.Game({ draw })`: true (headless): draw runs each advance on a stand-in renderer, its errors in game.errors\n- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n- `new E.Game({ live })`: true: a world that never stops for one player (online, a shared screen): a pause opens the menus over it, the scene's update goes on (game.paused says a menu is up) and the menu holds that player's input back\n- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n- `game.H`: the screen's height in game pixels (W is its width)\n- `game.W`: internal buffer size in pixels (HUD layout)\n- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (\n- `game.after(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true } (a site's clock, a watcher of the whole game: it outlives scenes, as a kept tween does). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are Strin\n- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); els\n- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the\n- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n- `game.every(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true }. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power '\n- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', '\n- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, \n- `game.hook(kind, fn)` †: a function of the game's own on the engine's clock: kind 'input' fn(dt) every step, paused or not, after the input is read (a host's guard, an idle timer); 'step' fn(dt) every update step in play, before the scene\n- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings')\n- `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse's point on the ground: [x, y] in world units (an array, not { x, y }), or null with no mouse over the canvas\n- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game \n- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n- `game.players` †: the players: [game.input (player 1), ...the ones who joined (game.allowJoin, game.addPlayer: section 10j)]; each an input with player (1..4), name ('P1'), color (E.PLAYER_COLORS) and device. A game of one: [game.input]\n- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits\n- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles\n- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n- `game.shaking` †: how hard the screen shakes now (0 still, up to 10): shake() adds to it and it dies away. A camera of the game's own (a 3D world's) shakes by it\n- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [t\n- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pa\n- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they\n- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides \n- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(ta\n- `game.upright`: true while the game's screen (r.W x r.H) is upright, taller than 1.3 times its width: a phone held upright, with expand: true (a fixed res keeps its own shape on any phone, so it is never upright there). A scene's camera.t\n- `game.viewports` †: a split screen's viewports ({ i, x, y, w, h, cam, focus(x, y, z), follow(target, { z }): game.splitScreen), or [] on one screen\n- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.\n- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n- `game.alpha = .5`: the fraction of a fixed step (0..1) of real time not yet run: interpolate drawing with it if motion must be smooth at any refresh rate. The accumulator starts half a step in, so frame times that wobble keep a steady ste\n- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n- `game.baseView` †: the chosen view without zoom and turn\n- `game.bootError = null`: the error a boot step failed with (the game did not start), or null\n- `game.booted`: a promise of the game once its boot (start's boot option, section 10c) has run and it has started: await it in a test whose boot steps return promises\n- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits\n- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n- `game.disposed = true`: true after dispose()\n- `game.drawEvery = 1`: 1, or 2 while a fast display (a 120 Hz phone) refreshes quicker than the game can draw (a frame's cost, or refreshes missed: its GPU behind): every other refresh is drawn, evenly, instead of random ones missed (the u\n- `game.errors = []`: the errors caught in update and draw, each { where, scene, message, stack }, once per kind\n- `game.fadeTime = .22`: seconds for the dithered fade between scenes (0 = instant cuts)\n- `game.fps = 60`: frames drawn per second over the last half second of real time\n- `game.fullscreen`: full screen: game.fullscreen.toggle(), .on, .supported\n- `game.gpu = null` †: the WebGPU lighting after enableGPU(), or null\n- `game.headless`: true for a game with no display (o.headless, or no DOM: Node)\n- `game.hitstop = 0`: seconds of hit-stop left (freeze: a scene's entry ends it)\n- `game.hz = 60`: the display's refresh rate, measured\n- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n- `game.live` †: true (o.live): a pause opens the menus over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player): paused is true and the scene's update goes on, the menu holding the player's own input back\n- `game.o`: the options the game was made with, the defaults filled in (view, res, expand, minW, minH, maxW, maxH, bg, step, input, loop, headless)\n- `game.particles`: dust, sparks, debris and text in the world\n- `game.pauseOverlay = true`: false hides the engine's PAUSED overlay (a game drawing its own pause screen)\n- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n- `game.r`: the renderer handed to draw(r) (null when headless)\n- `game.real = 0`: seconds the game has stepped through, pauses and hit-stop included (blinking HUD, notes)\n- `game.reduceMotion`: the player asked for less motion (prefers-reduced-motion): shake and flashes are softened\n- `game.scene = null`: the scene running\n- `game.sceneName = null`: its name ('main' for a game started with one scene)\n- `game.scenes = null`: the scenes given to start(), by name\n- `game.screen`: the low-resolution screen (its W, H, scale and buffer)\n- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n- `game.shell = null` †: the E.Shell over this game (an HTML interface: screens and HUD), or null\n- `game.stats`: per-frame timings (ms) and counts, for profiling overlays\n- `game.stats.updateMs`, `game.stats.renderMs`: milliseconds the last frame spent updating and drawing\n- `game.stats.frameMs`: the real time since the last frame\n- `game.stats.steps`: the updates it ran\n- `game.stats.items`: what it queued for drawing\n- `game.stats.actors`: actors drawn\n- `game.stats.culled`: actors skipped off screen\n- `game.time = 0`: game seconds since start (it stops during pause and hit-stop, and slows with timeScale)\n- `game.timeScale = 1`: game speed, 1 normal, .25 slow motion, 0 stopped (it outlives a scene change: a game's speed setting; restore a slow motion with a kept timer)\n- `game.view`: the view the game draws in now (the chosen one, with zoom and turn: read it, change it with setView)\n- `game.views`: views this game is designed for (view cycling and the checker use it). null = every view\n- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n- `E.PLAYER_COLORS: #4a8aff, #ff4a4a, #4ad860, #ffd040` †: the players' colors: player 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow (game.players)\n- `E.showError(title, e)`: show an error in the red box at the top of the page (the engine does it for errors in update and draw); a click hides it. Before play has started it shows on the boot screen instead, as a report a player can scree\n\n## 10b. Standard features\n\n- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a vali\n- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)\n- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n- `fullscreen.enter(quiet)`: go full screen. Browsers allow it only from a tap, click or key press: call it from one (a button, the menu, a key's update step). Returns a promise of whether it worked (a refusal is kept in failed and noted on\n- `fullscreen.exit()`: leave full screen (Escape and the phone's back gesture do it too)\n- `fullscreen.on`: true while the page is full screen\n- `fullscreen.standalone`: true when the page was opened from a home screen as an app (no browser bars: nothing to go full screen from)\n- `fullscreen.supported`: true where this page can go full screen (not an iPhone, which has Add to Home Screen instead; not a frame without permission; not headless)\n- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itself only while they haven't turned it off\n- `fullscreen.failed = null`: game: the game it serves; failed: why the last request was refused (a message), or null\n\n## 10c. Boot\n\n## 10k. Save slots\n\n- `new E.Saves(game, o) → saves` †: game.saves (made when first read): o is menu.saves ({ slots, version, migrate, write, read })\n- `saves.any` †: is there any save to load (a title's Continue)?\n- `saves.autosave(o)` †: save to the autosave (what write() gives)\n- `saves.copy(from, to)` †: copy a slot's save onto another (over what is there); false when the first is empty\n- `saves.erase(slot)` †: erase a slot\n- `saves.get(slot)` †: a slot's save as it is stored, checked: null when empty; { slot, damaged: true } when it can't be read; { newer: true } from a newer version of the game; else { slot, data, place, level, time, at (the date saved), ver\n- `saves.latest()` †: the most recent save that loads, or null (a title's Continue loads it)\n- `saves.list()` †: every slot, the autosave first: [{ slot, empty, place, level, time, at, damaged, newer }] (a menu's rows)\n- `saves.load(slot)` †: load a slot: its data brought up to this version (migrate), played set from it, read(save) called. Returns the save ({ data, place, level, time, slot }), or null when the slot is empty, damaged or from a newer versio\n- `saves.save(slot, o)` †: save to a slot (0.., or 'auto'): o ({ data, place, level }) or what write() gives. Returns true when it is kept (in storage, or for the visit when storage is blocked: blocked tells), false when there was nothing t\n- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game, menu: false)\n- `saves.blocked = false` †: storage refused a save (they last for the visit)\n- `saves.canSave = true` †: the pause menu offers Save (a JRPG turns it off away from save points)\n- `saves.game` †: its game\n- `saves.migrate` †: an older version's data brought up to this one (or null: kept as it is)\n- `saves.played = 0` †: seconds played in the game's scenes (paused time and titles left out), saved with each save and set by a load\n- `saves.read` †: what a load does with { data, place, level, time, slot }\n- `saves.slots` †: how many slots besides the autosave (3)\n- `saves.version` †: the game's version of its data (1), saved with each save\n- `saves.write` †: what a save holds ({ data, place, level })\n\n## 20. Audio\n\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (m\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, {\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playi\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017mKiE98DAigX3ahe81iuiy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
