{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjHUK8HANLJFirVZTQtL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Why Fit** is finished and everything in `PLAN.md` that can be done on this computer is done, but nobody has played it by hand. Balance was only checked with bots and stand-in devices, so how the lean feels on a real stick is still unknown. The sound was only measured as non-silent, never heard.\n\nThe title was picked at random from 50 candidates. All automated tests pass on the final build:\n\n| Test group | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Rules, including scripted five-day playthroughs (a good bot and a passive player) | 806 passed |\n| Browser (Chromium, plus WebKit and Firefox smoke tests) | 28 passed |\n| Hub, including the harness at all five settings | 6 passed |\n\nI first ran the hub group with a `limit=170` I had added to the harness URL. I removed it and reran the group so it matches the hub's own test, and it still passes.\n\nTwo tuning changes differ from the plan:\n- **Laundry Line:** it plays on easier balance numbers than the plan's rope row, because the average bot couldn't reach one star otherwise.\n- **Quiet Candle:** three stars now needs 85% instead of 90%, because the best bot topped out around 88%.\n\nThe game runs from a single HTML file with no network requests in standalone play. The About screen shows the same answers as `exit-interview.json`.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 22:14:47\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Sports and fitness minigame collection inside a light life-sim routine. The camera changes by activity:\n  - 2.5D brawler view in the studio\n  - three-quarter follow camera on the island\n  - Mode 7 behind the skier\n  - pseudo-3D road behind the runner\n  - steep custom view of the tray\n  - side view on the laundry line\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.89.0 (full engine, with its mode7, road, physics and rhythm modules), its Mocap player with a cut set of 23 clips, AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript on one global, bundled by a small Node script. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native look (3D skeleton puppets drawn as crisp pixel art at about 427×240), moved into bright daylight. Tare, Gerald, the Plate, mats, hoops, trays, gates and laundry are hand-drawn, plus a few CC0 store props.\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rigs driven by real motion capture, including yoga takes. A hand-posed IK layer handles side lunges, hula and step-ups; the rig wobbles about the feet with the player's lean, and Tare has spring squash.\n- **Audio:** All from code, no audio files. 13 original songs in the engine's chip synth, music at 10% by default, a click on the effects bus so the beat survives that volume, about 28 synthesized effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices made one oscillator per letter.\n- **Online:** AI Hub session protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1–4 players (Morning Class, with ghosts and the game's own card). Local Class for 2–4 on one screen. No other network.\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts and browsers on this computer: one command, `node tests/run.mjs`, covering rules, browser and hub groups; the hub harness at its five settings; screenshots for looks. Controller, touch, second player and sound were checked through stand-ins, not real hardware or real ears.\n- **Research:** Read the engine's handoff docs and kits and the AI Hub protocol README. No other research; the plan's recipes were followed.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14:\n  - Laundry Line too hard for the average bot\n  - its passive run outlasting the test guard\n  - Tray Service over too quickly\n  - the Rail's three stars out of reach\n  - the hub round sending no ghost data\n  - a mouse and keyboard source race\n  - a dark pause menu from a theme that never applied\n  - several layout overlaps found in screenshots\n- **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** 1. The hub round sent nothing during the harness's auto run, which would have kept the game out of AI Hub sessions.\n- **Most helpful test:** The bot sweep over every activity and tier against the star targets. It found every balance problem in one table and made tuning a matter of minutes.\n- **Least helpful test:** The determinism checks. They passed the first time and never failed; the engine's fixed step already guarantees them.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** Real motion capture playing on pixel-art rigs, and the headless `game.advance`. A whole five-day playthrough runs without a browser, with real taps, drawn on a stand-in.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. I guessed that the Shell was not yet made when my theme call ran, and a guard silently skipped the theme until a screenshot showed the dark menu. I also guessed a Backdrop sea layer would stay at the horizon; it filled the sky.\n- **Most important thing you had to build yourself?** The Lean: the balance model, the shared intent object that devices and bots both fill, and the Plate. Also the session flow that lets one scene serve story, Class, attract and the hub round.\n- **Hardest part of using the engine?** Layering my own wobble and hand poses over mocap, where each clip turns the rig by its own heading. The plan's recipes solved it; without them it would have taken a long time. Telling keyboard from mouse also needs game-side work.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** One patch: `E.Dialog`'s three hard-coded near-black text shadows now read `this.shadow`, because they smeared on a white dialog box. Nothing else.\n- **One thing you'd change?** Let `input.lastDevice` report mouse movement, so games do not need their own comparison to decide which device a prompt should show.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most?** The balance of freedom and a tested plan: every recipe marked as tested worked, so effort went into the game and not into the engine's traps.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan names engine v0.88.0 and the fetched handoff is v0.89.0, so the Dialog patch's line numbers differed slightly. \"Done\" also has to be judged without a person playing it.\n- **Hardest thing to do?** Judging feel with nobody to play it. Bots can prove a balance band, but not whether a lean feels good on a real stick.\n- **What would you change?** Add a short note on how to report what could not be felt or heard here, since the brief asks for a complete, polished game that no one on this computer can actually hold.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`; nothing is committed to git.\n- `WhyFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-221447.html`: the game\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WhyFit-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-221447/`: dev files; build with `node build.mjs`, test with `node tests/run.mjs [rules|browser|hub|all]`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1440,"cache_read_input_tokens":440989,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1440},"output_tokens":736,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a88ba50-da75-48c3-bde0-396ebfab72e9","uuid":"1c3d4c4e-24e1-4764-a1d6-e71e658f16e7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:11:09.046Z","request_id":"req_011CgjHUJb3S2B8bNj1c8KB8"}
